  "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x-\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x)+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x-\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=8 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5}{R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5} & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\u03d5 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \u03d5 & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{1}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
